s price cap the war in Ukraine  there's been heavy fighting again near  Bahamut in conditions described as  similar to those of World War One what's  at stake here  oh Frankly Speaking um if you look at  military analysis for example uh the  British Institute for the study of War  um those people who are professional  many of them ex-military uh they do not  understand what is actually the aim game  here for Russia Russia has been engaged  in and around bahmoud for several months  now it spends a lot of material and a  lot of Manpower uh on what seems to be  at best a tactical gain if this gain is  actually achieved uh I think what we did  what we're dealing with is with  um certainly lack of overall strategy uh  by the Russian commanders and by Putin  finally with regard to what they are  doing in the east of Ukraine this battle  is kind of fallen into a mosaic of  different small battles each of them is  not part of some kind of bigger  overarching plan and into that Mosaic  the Russian army is reportedly deploying  newly mobile civilized troops on the  front line will that change anything  I don't think so these people are well  for lack of better word not being too  politically correct I cannot afford it  for Putin because he actually cannot  bear let us say the Chinese board or the  Finnish body he can't transport the  whole Russian army to Ukraine so it has  to uh basically rely on untrained  concrets which are going to die on Mars  what is going to follow that and that is  my firm conviction is another  mobilization then another mobilization  it's another matter that it may be  conducted under some kind of other guys  but people will be  called up this will continue and there's  going to be more Russian deaths on the  front  okay Russia analyst Constantine Egger  thank you for that insight  thank you
 

